s \"$C$ only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345097 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115251 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "77d78574-a17c-5fc3-8c14-3aaec7753b3c" :knowledge-point/title "Transforming a 2x2 Matrix Into the Identity Matrix Using Row Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f9218e3-c5a3-504e-a8aa-4c7a7a889102" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343047} {:answer/id #uuid "76c4e5d3-bf62-5530-bdeb-b43a0ad3242b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343269} {:answer/id #uuid "97298d24-d2e5-5c5a-a83a-d3564f5c03e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343563} {:answer/id #uuid "c98ee987-6e30-5c28-bec6-af41c5351ce3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344008} {:answer/id #uuid "f8bb85dd-13a5-594c-b825-a8dcdeb42be7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344427}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f9218e3-c5a3-504e-a8aa-4c7a7a889102" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343047} :answer-field/id #uuid "18674c8a-58e7-537d-943e-33b31c03bca5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344532}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d8b28abc-ffbd-5f2b-b51a-6be08eb05498" :question/math-academy-id "q-75508" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are transformed into the $2\\times 2$ identity matrix after applying the row operations ${R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1}?$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the operations to each of the matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =I\\,✓ \\\\ B & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times  \\\\ C & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A$ only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345106 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115251 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "77d78574-a17c-5fc3-8c14-3aaec7753b3c" :knowledge-point/title "Transforming a 2x2 Matrix Into the Identity Matrix Using Row Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18956b73-de0f-5274-ad02-5066c0d2657b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342456} {:answer/id #uuid "2d821b77-91b8-542f-b59c-7b5c3ebcd4ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342620} {:answer/id #uuid "4b54bdc7-fb8f-5924-947c-0a287faf9bbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342858} {:answer/id #uuid "6348b2b2-8ae7-537e-909c-bb22df4127b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343078} {:answer/id #uuid "8bce61b5-2b6e-5b57-a1a3-1eef97b998b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343459}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8bce61b5-2b6e-5b57-a1a3-1eef97b998b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343459} :answer-field/id #uuid "40b7d972-25a9-5d09-b9bb-575a2e8ee618" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344596}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fd25b025-461f-556b-97f3-6071c19d59d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-75595" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are transformed into the $2\\times 2$ identity matrix after applying the row operations ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{2},\\,$ ${R}_{1}:=4{R}_{1},\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1}?$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{3}{4} & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the operations to each of the matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4} & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times  \\\\ B & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4} & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =I\\,✓ \\\\ C & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{3}{4} & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4} & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345107 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115251 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "77d78574-a17c-5fc3-8c14-3aaec7753b3c" :knowledge-point/title "Transforming a 2x2 Matrix Into the Identity Matrix Using Row Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59d338f8-b49b-5145-a7ef-1ae582d559cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342991} {:answer/id #uuid "92dcfa7a-f257-5a95-9893-29619578a1b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343522} {:answer/id #uuid "ea5a63f9-bd01-5a20-a2a4-c0a864ce4a0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344291} {:answer/id #uuid "f0b90c65-9660-55b2-9d45-57ee11b4570d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344351} {:answer/id #uuid "f3f172f0-b5cc-56f2-9005-34aef17200e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344385}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea5a63f9-bd01-5a20-a2a4-c0a864ce4a0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344291} :answer-field/id #uuid "d16727b3-99b0-547b-8c45-25b008d782b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344853}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1a52696c-f90c-56a7-94f5-6062f59efbae" :question/math-academy-id "q-75599" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are transformed into the $2\\times 2$ identity matrix after applying the row operations ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{2},\\,$ ${R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1},\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1}?$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the operations to each of the matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =I\\,✓ \\\\ B & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times  \\\\ C & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A$ only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345112 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119925 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "52a45814-d1e2-5948-b7bd-69dcaf6cd0eb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent and Dependent Sets Using Row Reduction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c30cee0-847d-5f40-ae43-134afe788817" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343010} {:answer/id #uuid "5e4716f8-1d1a-5bf6-a69d-02354cf35afa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343030} {:answer/id #uuid "6557bb3a-d669-5757-bc6e-f43fb74887f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343098} {:answer/id #uuid "7375a3b9-bff4-58a6-82e9-4245781699f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343237} {:answer/id #uuid "bdf19162-0472-5764-b148-da9e42eaf104" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343910}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e4716f8-1d1a-5bf6-a69d-02354cf35afa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343030} :answer-field/id #uuid "736c3de5-9a79-5920-82d2-3cbc3976ab40" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344700}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2f25df3d-4b0c-5b67-901d-8d878d2769ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-75655" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nAssume that ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly dependent.\n- The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent.\n- The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has only the trivial solution ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$" :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -6{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 12{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 12 & -4 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 12 & -4 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-4){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we conclude that the basic variables are ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$, while ${x}_{3}$ is the free variable. \n\nSince there is one free variable, the equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has an infinite number of nonzero solutions, which means that the vectors are linearly dependent. \n\nTherefore, statements II and III are false, while statement I is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345113 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119925 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "52a45814-d1e2-5948-b7bd-69dcaf6cd0eb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent and Dependent Sets Using Row Reduction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3da42d2a-fc8c-5560-a05e-8ebb08251ab7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342746} {:answer/id #uuid "428056c7-8c15-5083-b495-0a5d2db7568d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342788} {:answer/id #uuid "b05bd9d9-23cf-5926-8e64-3aa606f0b446" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343789} {:answer/id #uuid "f974dbb8-fa3b-57e5-b4a2-cbf533523461" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344432} {:answer/id #uuid "ff6841f4-2ac2-55ac-8a6c-00752966eb25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344488}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b05bd9d9-23cf-5926-8e64-3aa606f0b446" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343789} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c97eb9a-a992-5953-a80c-a71608c9beca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344686}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0d48ab70-ab46-5142-a432-382f1feb7bb9" :question/math-academy-id "q-75659" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nAssume that ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nII. The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has only the trivial solution ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n\nIII. The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent." :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the system is now\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}-2(0)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nand, substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-(0)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=0.\n$$\n\nSince ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0$, we get that the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, statements II and III are true, while statement I is false."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345114 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119925 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "52a45814-d1e2-5948-b7bd-69dcaf6cd0eb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent and Dependent Sets Using Row Reduction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11972855-80a9-5cac-9e70-8a4fcbdccf24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342393} {:answer/id #uuid "2bfeba34-6da2-5961-afdc-9d9564e3b098" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342608} {:answer/id #uuid "ac5b646e-db57-5a8d-9e82-9eb0862b3d92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343756} {:answer/id #uuid "acba01cf-19c6-57a0-8cf2-d050702ea3a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343758} {:answer/id #uuid "f9874691-41fb-52a1-b9c0-d3eb10d101ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344433}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac5b646e-db57-5a8d-9e82-9eb0862b3d92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343756} :answer-field/id #uuid "d156fe28-1c7f-5127-9119-2df01429f973" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344852}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8d177800-f6a6-59b3-bf6e-5adc772e358a" :question/math-academy-id "q-75660" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 13 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -7 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nAssume that ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent.\n\nII. The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIII. The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has an infinite number of nonzero solutions." :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{1}+13{x}_{2}-7{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+9{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 13 & -7 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 9 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 9 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the system is now\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}+(0)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nand, substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-3(0)+2(0)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=0.\n$$\n\nSince ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0$, we get that the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, statements II and III are false, while statement I is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345121 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105769 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "32467c2c-5605-50e9-b961-a29535a0effb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1299352e-d2ef-5e23-a6a9-e3484a823eab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342402} {:answer/id #uuid "6945182e-95b7-5e01-ab43-e486bfa69c53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343137} {:answer/id #uuid "cab42dea-25c4-565b-904b-b7e77ae2c127" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344021} {:answer/id #uuid "e402b43c-fdcf-51ca-9fd7-ca29f619233d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344240} {:answer/id #uuid "ea45b5bf-09d1-5fd5-a6ce-b44ab9b5150c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344290}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cab42dea-25c4-565b-904b-b7e77ae2c127" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344021} :answer-field/id #uuid "200fb1e1-dd6d-5c55-afee-57f8fc7300f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344549}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7a5c031b-68ff-5d30-80f0-79dec42f9067" :question/math-academy-id "q-75729" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- If $v$ is an eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ then $v$ is also an eigenvector of the matrix ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to $\\lambda =-2.$\n- An eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ is also an eigenvector of the inverse ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the same eigenvalue $\\lambda =1.$\n- If $v$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2,$ then $7v$ is also an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ With that in mind, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement is false. By the definition of an eigenvector, we have \n\n$$\nAv=2v.\n$$\n\n \nMultiplying it by ${A}^{-1}$ from the left, we obtain \n\n$$\nv=2{A}^{-1}v.\n$$\n\nDividing both sides by $2$, we get \n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}v=\\frac{1}{2}v.\n$$\n\n \nSo, $v$ is an eigenvector  of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n**Note:** In general, an eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to an eigenvalue $\\lambda $ is an eigenvector of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\frac{1}{\\lambda }.$\n\n- Statement II is true. According to the statement above, an eigenvector of $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ is an eigenvector of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{1}=1.$\n\n- Statement III is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(7v)=7Av=7(2v)=2(7v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince, $A(7v)=2(7v),$ and $7v\\ne 0,$ this implies that $7v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =2.$\n\n**Note:** In general, any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is again an eigenvector, and the corresponding eigenvalue is the same.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345193 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078487 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f00fd821-a5d4-5844-9db5-795b9e321b94" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Largest Intervals of Continuity of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5900b2ad-c394-58a3-8661-86744ec49f7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342986} {:answer/id #uuid "71ec907a-fb23-50c4-8b1c-c42f54b462fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343223} {:answer/id #uuid "80e38216-9a2e-56cf-9bab-79d5b46a98fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343349} {:answer/id #uuid "b1ee4e70-3d9b-51db-b23d-bc1a59a3bace" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343800} {:answer/id #uuid "f0ea78ef-a8ff-5431-b650-ef064402d8dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344358}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5900b2ad-c394-58a3-8661-86744ec49f7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342986} :answer-field/id #uuid "4cf7e708-707f-5e09-821c-37a874528e08" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344623}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ca01d454-a476-5d37-9896-aa972462aa23" :question/math-academy-id "q-76208" :question/problem "![](images/ab/abfd15c8ed134a30ad719f6a857268427511e7f1f15ecfe0d27e5026b5401a83.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ above. On which of the following intervals is the function continuous? \n\n- $x\\in (-\\infty ,-2)$\n- $x\\in [-2,3)$\n- $x\\in [3,\\infty )$\n\nYou may assume that all points of discontinuity of $f(x)$ are shown in the diagram." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each statement.\n\n- The function is continuous on the open interval $(-\\infty ,-2).$\n\n- The function is continuous on $[-2,3).$ It is continuous on $(-2,3)$ and also right continuous at $x=-2.$\n\n- The function is continuous on $[3,\\infty ).$ It is continuous on $(3,\\infty )$ and also right continuous at $x=3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345195 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078487 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f00fd821-a5d4-5844-9db5-795b9e321b94" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Largest Intervals of Continuity of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26764b32-2c60-587f-b8c5-37fa19d94968" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function is not continuous on any of the given intervals" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342564} {:answer/id #uuid "2efc0ccc-6f9e-51e2-9559-c2a8b34966df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342632} {:answer/id #uuid "a45ef78d-716e-51d4-a3ff-b11c8025f4f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343688} {:answer/id #uuid "b3675348-452e-5b76-9