the Largest Intervals of Continuity of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1075d718-40b0-5742-b4f2-dbdbdf2e06b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342380} {:answer/id #uuid "15769205-ba90-5639-abae-d9422352b485" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function is not continuous on any of the given intervals" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342429} {:answer/id #uuid "39cc683a-00e8-585f-91ea-14d5c5a1dfa0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342715} {:answer/id #uuid "9c5540c3-8c22-5a59-9e92-56b97da12a15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343616} {:answer/id #uuid "d40e7fd5-7f34-56f0-93bc-e8e4ed2a8dc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344099}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1075d718-40b0-5742-b4f2-dbdbdf2e06b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342380} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f8fe9a0-755d-5543-a40d-443c23f5ea83" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344629}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a8275053-44d7-522b-8662-3358231e9c18" :question/math-academy-id "q-76218" :question/problem "![](images/18/186ac941080da34b7c149534a2e255852241b28855c0ef15db2beee2d692007a.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ above. On which of the following intervals is the function continuous? \n\n- $x\\in (-\\infty ,3]$\n- $x\\in (-\\infty ,5)$\n- $x\\in (4,6)$\n\nYou may assume that all points of discontinuity of $f(x)$ are shown in the diagram." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each statement.\n\n- The function is *not* continuous on $(-\\infty ,3].$ Although the function is continuous on $(-\\infty ,3),$ it is not left-continuous at $x=3.$\n\n- The function is *not* continuous on $(-\\infty ,5)$ because it contains a discontinuity at $x=3.$\n\n- The function is continuous on $(4,6).$ It is continuous on $[3,\\infty )$ and this contains the interval $(4,6).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345292 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096691 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c015dcaa-2155-5d80-985b-21924de92b28" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vector Equalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12f5e655-d76e-5618-a17f-2483870f80ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342404} {:answer/id #uuid "4a960852-e36e-5de9-accc-56acd04910ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342852} {:answer/id #uuid "6234df6c-15c7-5877-b0fb-75883f2806a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343071} {:answer/id #uuid "df4e9b9c-c909-5afd-bf7b-e6148fe13a5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344196} {:answer/id #uuid "f6e83d4b-c078-5faf-ac16-d2afb50445ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344411}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f6e83d4b-c078-5faf-ac16-d2afb50445ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344411} :answer-field/id #uuid "467ab3d5-e2b3-5afa-a2e2-f13c2a37966a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344610}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "43864da8-ac06-5dee-a580-c31c2739a8e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-77389" :question/problem "![](images/e1/e17584d96cb8d83ccb1abfbd16c4117f58639b7cd3462ee50ad3f31e4eabe279.png)\n\nIn figure $ABCDEF$ shown above, which of the following equalities are true?\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{BC}-\\overset{\\to }{DC}=\\overset{\\to }{BD}$\n- $\\overset{\\to }{BA}+\\overset{\\to }{AE}-\\overset{\\to }{FE}=\\overset{\\to }{BF}$\n- $\\overset{\\to }{CD}-\\overset{\\to }{ED}-\\overset{\\to }{FE}=\\overset{\\to }{FC}$" :question/worked-solution "- Equality I is true. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{BC}-\\overset{\\to }{DC} & =\\overset{\\to }{BC}+\\overset{\\to }{CD} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{BD}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Equality II is true. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{BA}+\\overset{\\to }{AE}-\\overset{\\to }{FE} & =\\overset{\\to }{BA}+(\\overset{\\to }{AE}+\\overset{\\to }{EF}) \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{BA}+\\overset{\\to }{AF} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{BF}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Equality III is false. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{CD}-\\overset{\\to }{ED}-\\overset{\\to }{FE} & =(\\overset{\\to }{CD}+\\overset{\\to }{DE})+\\overset{\\to }{EF} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{CE}+\\overset{\\to }{EF} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{CF} \\\\  & \\ne \\overset{\\to }{FC}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345293 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119790 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd9b0f65-d8a3-57c4-88c5-2738b0938c0d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Coefficient in a 2x2 Linear System Using Cramer's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25f96b37-25e2-57e5-a9bd-ce85f77ef622" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342559} {:answer/id #uuid "6810a70d-fc25-5dc9-9176-1e77c5c64ef1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343123} {:answer/id #uuid "9b153830-307e-510b-95ec-885456562d1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343605} {:answer/id #uuid "d9f2fb38-44b4-51a3-ae94-6982dfb498bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344152} {:answer/id #uuid "f051a762-fe84-5e5b-a57f-76839faef4a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344349}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d9f2fb38-44b4-51a3-ae94-6982dfb498bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344152} :answer-field/id #uuid "6cc90745-e978-5189-b064-c8cfba27da07" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344687}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "dfe3c702-6424-5bb5-8a56-52b5a6e22724" :question/math-academy-id "q-77400" :question/problem "Let $⟨x,-2⟩$ be the solution of the system of equations below. \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-y=2 \\\\ 2x+y=k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following equations could be solved to find the value of $k?$\n\n- $|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & k\\end{aligned}|=-6$\n- $|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & k\\end{aligned}|=-12$\n- $|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & k\\end{aligned}|=3$" :question/worked-solution "The determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1+2 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the value of $y$ is given by $y=\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D},$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{y} & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & k\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D} \\\\ -2 & =\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & k\\end{aligned}|}{3} \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & k\\end{aligned}| & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345294 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119790 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd9b0f65-d8a3-57c4-88c5-2738b0938c0d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Coefficient in a 2x2 Linear System Using Cramer's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20bb0734-b113-5738-811e-5ed128d214ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342507} {:answer/id #uuid "27742a41-54e2-59b5-b786-db849377783d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342573} {:answer/id #uuid "44122616-845c-5061-9e80-69872573e8f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342803} {:answer/id #uuid "8d84791f-2e4f-5e1a-8d1d-454662569e37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343476} {:answer/id #uuid "b6e5462b-343b-5798-95b7-33b70f0f69a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343841}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d84791f-2e4f-5e1a-8d1d-454662569e37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343476} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c1e02f2-9665-5243-aedc-17c100ebdba4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344684}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a75a54e5-59f0-5536-9005-ac12974e1a69" :question/math-academy-id "q-77401" :question/problem "Let $⟨x,1⟩$ be the solution of the system of equations below. \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3x-y=k \\\\ x+4y=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following equations could be solved to find the value of $k?$\n\n- $|\\begin{aligned}3 & k \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}|=5$\n- $|\\begin{aligned}3 & k \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}|=-7$\n- $|\\begin{aligned}3 & k \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}|=13$" :question/worked-solution "The determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =12+1 \\\\  & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the value of $y$ is given by $y=\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D},$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{y} & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & k \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D} \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}3 & k \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}|}{13} \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}3 & k \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}| & =13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345308 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119931 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6a3d254-c96f-5aa0-9db6-a587eb7cb2de" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Expression Involving Given Determinants and Matrix Types"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42e2d978-2c80-5bfd-8184-7620c86b4d9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342796} {:answer/id #uuid "87caf754-01fe-5135-8e0f-3937db98fe01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343413} {:answer/id #uuid "aaac6d10-cd8e-529d-b81e-8bac65acd12c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343740} {:answer/id #uuid "df109891-92e9-56d1-95e6-b29b4c4c96cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344195} {:answer/id #uuid "f39fd878-ef99-5884-b619-883485188f80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344383}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aaac6d10-cd8e-529d-b81e-8bac65acd12c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343740} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ae19f3c-dbd3-5456-af19-7dc1239a420f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344540}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "49749c9e-4840-5ef3-a87f-c1ce7810b100" :question/math-academy-id "q-77503" :question/problem "Given that $det(A)=2$, which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $-2\\text{det}({A}^{T})-3\\text{det}(I)=7$\n- $4\\text{det}({A}^{T})=2$\n- $\\text{det}({A}^{T})-\\text{det}(I)=-\\frac{1}{2}$" :question/worked-solution "From the properties of determinants, we know that \n\n$$\n\\text{det}({A}^{T})=\\text{det}(A)=2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ndet(I)=1.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have,\n\n$$\n-2\\text{det}({A}^{T})-3\\text{det}(I)=-2\\cdot 2-3\\cdot 1=-7\\ne 7.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n4\\text{det}({A}^{T})=4\\cdot 2=8\\ne 2.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\text{det}({A}^{T})-\\text{det}(I)=2-1=1\\ne -\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"None.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345345 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b4b32f9-8362-5c81-a1fe-4457d91ae571" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Functions That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13c9b65e-8aab-58e4-9ff8-f146c4b1dfb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342412} {:answer/id #uuid "bc16e6f4-a9a7-5ef2-a3e8-5f2cc8fd2073" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343890} {:answer/id #uuid "dcbb9443-92d4-5c38-8324-856eb59d6047" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344177} {:answer/id #uuid "e96dfec3-2242-5abf-9843-aa4fbc07fe3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344281} {:answer/id #uuid "edf0bc6b-95d8-5574-ae34-66cc789aa7f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344329}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13c9b65e-8aab-58e4-9ff8-f146c4b1dfb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342412} :answer-field/id #uuid "35f8c506-59e5-5df7-b6f5-b6b5ef1620ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344587}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b0acb71b-a2ed-51d8-8bca-93474ee1c192" :question/math-academy-id "q-77864" :question/problem "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-2,2]?$\n\n- $f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}$\n- $g(x)=|{x}^{2}+1|$\n- $h(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)$" :question/worked-solution "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-2,2],$ the function must be continuous on $[-2,2]$ and differentiable on $(-2,2).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ In particular, it is continuous on $[-2,2]$ and differentiable on $(-2,2).$ Therefore, $f(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $g(x)=|{x}^{2}+1|$ is equivalent to $p(x)={x}^{2}+1,$ so it is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ In particular, it is continuous on $[-2,2]$ and differentiable on $(-2,2).$ Therefore, $g(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $h(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ In particular, it is continuous on $[-2,2]$ and differentiable on $(-2,2).$ Therefore, $h(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nTherefore, the functions I, II, and III satisfy the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[-2,2].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348109 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115300 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "68350e0d-84ca-5b50-b05a-1bca3ceea40f" :knowledge-point/title "Transforming a 3x3 Matrix Into the Identity Matrix Using Row Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "185619d8-1b91-5d2f-af5e-dee1634d5555" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345565} {:answer/id #uuid "44f107dd-253f-5b2e-bda0-164d1239fdcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345949} {:answer/id #uuid "515c1c66-f6e4-50c0-b52d-198cd74b8a3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346047} {:answer/id #uuid "8d916295-fa1a-5ff5-ba9b-5d907aac02a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346582} {:answer/id #uuid "e3b4ad68-2c01-51fc-89a3-6a26083ddcde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347353}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e3b4ad68-2c01-51fc-89a3-6a26083ddcde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347353} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1aaa798-00b3-55fa-9339-75ae07399b92" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347971}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ad44d2b4-38e0-5faa-8080-32a8feda902f" :question/math-academy-id "q-78772" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are transformed into the $3\\times 3$ identity matrix after applying the row operations ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2}?$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the operations to each of the matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times  \\\\ B & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =I\\,✓ \\\\ C & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348154 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184151 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "437c168a-6799-5d13-bd9b-031290a84da6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intervals of Concavity of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d1ea255-7dc4-54d4-9172-7acf08d05cd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345453} {:answer/id #uuid "b2253313-0064-565d-aab1-ac081b160a04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346906} {:answer/id #uuid "b4c2d995-4b9c-5b32-8ff4-4cd6a59aa059" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346926} {:answer/id #uuid "ccb08342-c5da-554a-bb78-0962f941c5a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347141} {:answer/id #uuid "da3e0bea-256a-5720-8bd7-3b470182a9c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347270}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d1ea255-7dc4-54d4-9172-7acf08d05cd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345453} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0666484-0b92-5c23-994f-2dff9365864f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347966}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c3b9e269-d2c5-5e09-8e6b-2689345ee964" :question/math-academy-id "q-79039" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}+5x.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(2,\\infty )$\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(2,\\infty )$\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(-\\infty ,2)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}-12x+5 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =6x-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x-12 & >0\\,⟹\\,x>2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the function is concave up on $(2,\\infty ).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality $6x-12<0,$ which has the solution $(-\\infty ,2).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348159 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184151 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "437c168a-6799-5d13-bd9b-031290a84da6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intervals of Concavity of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06f70b66-96e9-5aae-93da-647ff3e21f32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345413} {:answer/id #uuid "1ccbca84-26a7-531b-bc40-848a39c9d145" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345601} {:answer/id #uuid "820bdb8d-1b1f-5a23-91ee-e42c0fd7b00c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346488} {:answer/id #uuid "964ca8d1-bc5e-5d1c-a5e6-7d742ff7cbd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346665} {:answer/id #uuid "b684115e-c70b-5e4f-9a6a-91ed32fef39b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346942}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b684115e-c70b-5e4f-9a6a-91ed32fef39b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346942} :answer-field/id #uuid "f84b80f4-3cda-509e-bb73-3c54426e4477" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348041}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d60b7843-0bd3-5e4e-8b85-becdd36b22ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-79047" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=2+3x-{e}^{-x}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(-\\infty ,\\infty )$\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(0,\\infty )$\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(-\\infty ,0)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =3+{e}^{-x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{-x}>0$ for every real value of $x,$ it follows that $-{e}^{-x}<0$ for every real value of $x.$ Therefore, ${f}^{″}(x)$ is never positive, so there are no intervals where $f(x)$ is concave up.\n\nSince ${f}^{″}(x)<0$ for every real value of $x,$ the function is concave down on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348182 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087702 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c6056ee-15f3-5b5c-8a14-173ea1da1381" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Singular Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "406edcd7-dd0d-553f-94d4-59c097c98af4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$G$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345904} {:answer/id #uuid "465f4a2b-19c0-58df-8ab0-2cc0a8ebc295" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$F$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345964} {:answer/id #uuid "47dcbc0e-948f-533c-8be5-738050dd7dae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$F$ and $G$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345967} {:answer/id #uuid "c7f84565-bd95-5131-8e6d-cd3a127bd0b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D$ and $F$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347089} {:answer/id #uuid "c9ba910b-46fc-5621-8530-c677d52695b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D$ and $G$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347109}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "465f4a2b-19c0-58df-8ab0-2cc0a8ebc295" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$F$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345964} :answer-field/id #uuid "723ed971-5225-51a2-a32f-826e5f345808" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347809}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6cd97c58-6193-5fe7-8948-41d6f4bfcd71" :question/math-academy-id "q-7923" :question/problem "Which of the matrices below does **not** have an inverse?\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{aligned}],\\,F=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{aligned}],\\,G=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "- For the matrix $D$, we have\n\n$$\ndet(D)=3\\cdot 6-6\\cdot 2=6.\n$$\n\nBecause $det(D)\\ne 0,$ we conclude that $D$ is invertible.\n\n- For the matrix $F$, we have\n\n$$\ndet(F)=6\\cdot 6-6\\cdot 6=0.\n$$\n\nBecause $det(F)=0,$ we conclude that $F$ is not invertible.\n\n- For the matrix $G$, we have\n\n$$\ndet(G)=(-2)\\cdot 4-4\\cdot 2=-16.\n$$\n\nBecause $det(G)\\ne 0,$ we conclude that $G$ is invertible.\n\nTherefore, only $F$ does not have an inverse."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348208 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124629 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bb90e5b9-03fe-51cf-bc16-a293befacebc" :knowledge-point/title "Сompleting a Sentence Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "097b8a1b-b44d-5e76-97c8-b4c1a59a0e59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345426} {:answer/id #uuid "6baff456-609b-5210-afb9-ca38e5adabcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346284} {:answer/id #uuid "bbc14084-3088-5b8a-ba2d-7b0e0a1aadc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346981} {:answer/id #uuid "cdb60f3a-a601-548c-bc1f-20c9cc148f2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347151} {:answer/id #uuid "ffc15575-a017-565f-8f76-b1cadb4fc7dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347601}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bbc14084-3088-5b8a-ba2d-7b0e0a1aadc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346981} :answer-field/id #uuid "458244de-522e-5387-a418-9f5ed1cbeef6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347720}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e25ee2d5-6236-5cb7-ae46-36798fde6f33" :question/math-academy-id "q-79334" :question/problem "Consider the following sentence:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A)=0,$ then  $\\text{__________}\\,.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\nI. $A$ is **not** invertible\n\nII. $\\text{rank}(A)=n$\n\nIII. $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n}$" :question/worked-solution "The invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the options separately.\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A)=0,$ then $A$ is **not** invertible.* $\\,\\times $\n\nBy the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(A)=0,$ we must have that $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A)=0,$ then $\\text{rank}(A)=n.$* $\\,✓$ \n\nIndeed, since $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{rank}(A)=\\text{dim(Col}(A))=n.$\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A)=0,$ then $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n}.$* $\\,✓$\n\nIndeed, since $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348227 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059789 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4b612ef-ce7f-5783-848d-d8ef210c9f47" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Argand Diagram Corresponding to a Complex Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c1e4021-3396-5bc6-83ea-5a27c6cabe76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345596} {:answer/id #uuid "3dea748a-d0d4-5afc-925f-273d667f091e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345875} {:answer/id #uuid "7243bf19-b992-547d-9233-c657639a06c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346361} {:answer/id #uuid "d13f5329-2ec1-5018-9bce-9ec236dfe908" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347185} {:answer/id #uuid "d7dc2a08-b945-5e4e-9cd1-679eca7a90c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347252}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d13f5329-2ec1-5018-9bce-9ec236dfe908" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347185} :answer-field/id #uuid "ced0da57-29f7-5224-b3bb-c98987c02847" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347960}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8289966f-f636-5804-9edf-c054d1b5acd5" :question/math-academy-id "q-79457" :question/problem "Which of the following numbers lie in the second quadrant in the corresponding Argand diagram?\n\n- $-2-2\\text{i}$\n- $-2+\\text{i}$\n- $-1+2\\text{i}$" :question/worked-solution "- The complex number $-2-2\\text{i}$ has the real part $x=-2$ and the imaginary part $y=-2.$ So, it corresponds to the point $(-2,-2)$, which lies in the third quadrant.\n\n- The complex number $-2+\\text{i}$ has the real part $x=-2$ and the imaginary part $y=1.$ So, it corresponds to the point $(-2,1)$, which lies in the second quadrant.\n\n- The complex number $-1+2\\text{i}$ has the real part $x=-1$ and the imaginary part $y=2.$ So, it corresponds to the point $(-1,2)$, which lies in the second quadrant.\n\n![](images/9f/9f2e7234ba52d14098e0ecfc9695181b425764c110995b76b4b0efddf206252c.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348228 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059789 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4b612ef-ce7f-5783-848d-d8ef210c9f47" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Argand Diagram Corresponding to a Complex Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d4636db-db02-5863-92b4-8abb34eb2771" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346581} {:answer/id #uuid "a4bb8bff-0b5e-51a9-8760-878220ba9463" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346788} {:answer/id #uuid "c1f68a00-1742-54f4-9473-c700f4f12b30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347028} {:answer/id #uuid "ca930a2a-126d-5edd-aa68-a071673eb20c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347117} {:answer/id #uuid "e2c0088a-60c2-538f-9ba7-d71e584516e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347346}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca930a2a-126d-5edd-aa68-a071673eb20c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347117} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd69c411-1af3-5cc9-9bc9-5772e6c3d56f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348048}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1861228f-d3da-56c4-af5a-dc5d012713d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-79458" :question/problem "Which of the following numbers lie in the fourth quadrant in an Argand diagram?\n\n- $\\frac{3}{2}-\\text{i}$\n- $-\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}$\n- $\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{3}{2}\\text{i}$" :question/worked-solution "- The complex number $\\frac{3}{2}-\\text{i}$ has the real part $x=\\frac{3}{2}$ and the imaginary part $y=-1.$ So, it corresponds to the point $(\\frac{3}{2},-1)$, which lies in the fourth quadrant.\n\n- The complex number $-\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}$ has the real part $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and the imaginary part $y=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, it corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{1}{2})$, which lies in the third quadrant.\n\n- The complex number $\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{3}{2}\\text{i}$ has the real part $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ and the imaginary part $y=\\frac{3}{2}.$ So, it corresponds to the point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2})$, which lies in the first quadrant.\n\n![](images/d7/d7390ddcc8d338d2fd46bc12b0a4c65f8e6bf6e8c4f6c9fa802e2acea159416b.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}]}
